e general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=5.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{4(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 5 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=5{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼5{e}^{4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365937 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365937 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365937 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365937 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365937 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa3102c8-b53d-52e0-8f47-cacae0ffde3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365938 #edb/ref 1080 "5{e}^{4x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365938 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365938 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "91505a79-2ea5-54d7-95d8-90e5cea46420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365960 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $6$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below repre